WEBVTT

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:52.560
Amphibians were the first backbone animals to leave the water and colonise the land.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.760
Today there are some 6,000 species of them and new ones are constantly being discovered.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:14.840
We may not often see them but during the breeding season we certainly hear them.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:27.880
Choruses like this ensure that we are well aware of frogs and toads.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:33.240
But there are other kinds of amphibians that don't make themselves so obvious.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:40.760
Newts and their close relatives the salamanders and even ones that have completely lost their

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.160
legs.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.720
But all amphibians have one thing in common, a moist skin.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:55.360
If that dries they die and dealing with that danger dominates their lives.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:07.880
How are they to survive away from water?

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:13.040
Four hundred million years ago the only backbone animals on the earth were fish.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.240
The land was empty except for insects and other invertebrates.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:23.840
But then one of those fish managed to haul itself out of the water and up onto the land.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.720
You can see what sort of creature that might have been if you go to north-east Australia.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:35.200
There the rivers only too often dry up.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:43.360
But one remarkable ancient and extraordinary fish managed to survive because it has a rare

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.800
talent for a fish.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:53.080
It has lungs and can breathe air and there's one at my feet right here.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:58.720
Fossils just like it date from precisely the time when the great invasion of the land took

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.360
place.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:06.200
On occasion it rises to the surface and gulps air.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:13.500
The air goes into a pouch that opens from its throat where the oxygen from it is absorbed.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:19.280
This is a lung fish.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:25.520
It punts itself along the river bottom using two pairs of fleshy muscular fins placed low

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:32.000
on its body just like simple legs.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:39.060
Sometime around 360 million years ago one of its remote ancestors used such limb-like

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:48.240
fins to push itself up onto the land.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:54.560
That pioneer may have looked much like this strange monster that haunts the waterways

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.320
of Japan.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:05.240
It's the giant salamander, the biggest of all living amphibians, that grows to a metre

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:12.160
or more in length.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.680
It too has lungs and breathes air.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:22.040
But even so it almost never leaves the water.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:27.240
Males make their dens in both natural and man-made retreats in the riverbanks and defend

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.160
them against all other males.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:50.160
A newcomer arrives looking for a breeding den of his own.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:05:19.640
It won't be here.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:26.320
The resident male has good reason to be so defensive.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:33.240
He's guarding a batch of eggs left by a female who visited him a few days earlier.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.760
Like fish eggs, amphibian eggs have no protective shell.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:41.140
They can only develop in moisture of some kind and amphibians, no matter where they

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:47.660
live, must find ways to provide that.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:54.840
The alpine newt lived on land for about half the year, hunting for slugs and worms.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:05.600
In winter they lie dormant beneath the snow, but come the spring they get the urge to breed.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:19.720
A female is swollen with eggs and needs to lay, so she has to go back to water.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:24.480
And there a male is awaiting her.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:34.520
He has already developed his breeding colours and knows how to flaunt them to impress her.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:53.560
He wafts a pheromone, a sexual stimulant, towards her with beats of his tail.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:07:06.680
She senses it through her nostrils.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:15.280
She tastes it in her mouth.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:21.840
Having caught her interest, he turns and moves away from her.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.640
His genital opening is greatly swollen and from it comes a small white capsule.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:31.680
It's a packet of sperm.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:39.680
The female, led by the male, walks directly over it.

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:46.600
He stops and so does she, with her genital opening exactly above the sperm packet.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:52.200
And she picks it up.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:08:04.120
So, as in many fish, mating occurs with little or no physical contact between the two partners.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:13.120
Two or three days later, she begins to lay.

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:28.560
Each of her eggs is deposited individually.

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:34.520
As an egg emerges, she wraps the leaf around it with her hind legs and then holds it there

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:37.640
while the edges bond.

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:43.320
She will lay several eggs a day for week after week until eventually she may have produced

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:50.520
several hundred.

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:53.640
But all this has to be done in water.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:09:00.820
She has still not broken her link with her fishy ancestry.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:06.520
In North America, in the eastern half of the country, there are many kinds of small salamanders,

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:14.800
only a few inches long, that have taken one further step away from the aquatic life.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:22.040
In spring, the woodlands are drenched in rain and suddenly, in response, an amphibian army

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:26.920
appears among the leaf litter.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:32.960
Marbled salamanders.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:34.640
First to emerge are the males.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:38.520
They are in search of females.

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:43.600
They have spent the winter deep in the damp leaf litter, breathing by absorbing oxygen

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:46.320
from the air through their moist skins.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:49.320
For them, the land is truly home.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:55.400
If they were submerged in water for any length of time, they might well drown.

00:09:55.400 --> 00:10:05.400
Nonetheless, their courtship techniques are much the same as those used in water by newts.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:18.680
The males produce pheromones that excite the females.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:22.480
They deposit capsules of sperm on the damp ground.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:29.480
And the females crawl over them and take them in.

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:35.640
In due course, each female lays her soft-skinned eggs on the ground and stays beside them on

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:37.280
guard.

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:43.160
Here, it's damp enough to prevent her eggs from drying, and they're already developing

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:48.040
rapidly.

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:56.520
Eventually, the continuing rains flood the woodland floor.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:11:02.840
But now, the females' needs and those of her eggs are exactly opposite.

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:15.040
They will need water in order to breathe, but she could drown in it, so she has to leave.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:19.680
The young inside their capsules are developing into creatures fundamentally different from

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:23.920
their parents, a form that is characteristic of amphibians.

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:26.880
They are becoming tadpoles.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:33.760
They swim free, equipped with feathery gills that enable them to extract oxygen from the

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:34.760
water.

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:37.520
They are truly aquatic creatures.

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:49.640
But they have front legs as well as gills.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:55.240
And within days, they develop back legs as well.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:59.600
As time passes, they grow stronger.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:04.920
Their gills wither and disappear, and at last, they are miniature versions of their parents

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:12.560
and are ready to leave the water forever and to start on their land-living lives.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:18.960
But what tempted those ancient fish to leave the water in the first place?

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:21.080
Food.

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:27.480
When the first amphibians moved out of water, the land was already swarming with insects.

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:36.080
And the amphibians have evolved a special weapon with which to catch them.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:46.440
Salamanders, however, have not yet developed the athleticism needed for a high-speed chase

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:48.000
under lightning pounce.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:13:13.480
Their hunts are rather solemn, sedate affairs.

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:18.520
A simple contraction of the muscles surrounding the tongue is all that's needed to shoot it

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:25.000
forward.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:29.960
Some salamanders have a tongue that is about three-quarters the length of the body, but

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:41.680
most species have to get pretty close to their prey if they're to catch it.

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:47.120
Although the adult marble salamander lives entirely on land, it nonetheless needed water

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:49.560
at the very beginning of its life.

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:53.720
But there are other species of salamander in North America that have managed to break

00:13:53.720 --> 00:14:04.440
even that link with their distant aquatic past.

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:07.000
This is a gold mine.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:14.120
The people who dug it found nothing, but biologists who came later found gold of their own special

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:24.100
kind.

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:29.220
They discovered a colony of a species called the slimy salamander that could be properly

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:33.760
observed throughout the summer when normally they're hidden in the leaf litter.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:45.480
They were all females and their behavior proved to be very surprising indeed.

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:52.660
These salamanders come down in early summer in about June and will travel several hundred

00:14:52.660 --> 00:15:01.480
meters down along this mine shaft to exactly the same ledge within an inch or so that they

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:08.960
used the previous year and they have been seen doing that for at least five or six years.

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:10.800
And they don't eat.

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:17.640
They will stay down here for six or seven months sustained only by the food reserves

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:23.280
that they've accumulated in their fat tails.

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:31.440
And here there is permanent moisture, however hot and dry it gets outside.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:36.380
The salamanders clearly prefer to cluster together close to one another for the rock

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:42.320
walls of the mine shaft elsewhere are totally uninhabited.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:49.040
However this open plan way of life, while it's clearly very successful, nonetheless

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:52.320
comes at a price.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:56.400
Some of the females here are up to no good.

00:15:56.400 --> 00:16:01.840
They failed to fatten up enough during the spring and they're hungry and in search of

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:04.080
a good meal.

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:12.600
And the eggs and young of their other salamanders will do very well.

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:18.520
To see exactly what these creatures are doing we need to turn off our torches and turn on

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:28.000
the infrared camera.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:41.960
Here comes one of those marauding females.

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:47.240
She must have located this mother guarding her eggs by smell for all this is going on

00:16:47.240 --> 00:17:08.520
in total darkness.

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:17.760
So some amphibians when needs be are neither sluggish, insensitive nor lacking in maternal

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:21.480
concern.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:27.040
And mother wins the day.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:32.600
The salamanders need to keep moist means that they seldom come out into the open but find

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:42.080
their prey by pushing through the leaf litter and to do that it helps to be slim, very slim.

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:47.040
Legs are less in the way if they're small.

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:58.760
And one great group of buttering amphibians has lost its legs altogether.

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:04.560
You might think that this was a giant earthworm but if you picked it up you would immediately

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:08.320
realize it's got a strong firm backbone.

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:11.800
It's a Sicilian.

00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:18.480
Sicilians are found in almost all rainforests but they are seldom seen for they spend nearly

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:22.760
all their lives underground.

00:18:22.760 --> 00:18:34.920
The female having produced her young stays in her nest chamber to protect them.

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:36.640
Sicilian eyes are rudimentary.

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:39.560
They're covered in skin and scarcely function.

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:47.240
In the darkness underground however the animals have no need for them.

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:52.960
The young enthusiastically lick a secretion from a gland at the end of their mother's

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:57.560
tail and their constant hunger seems to be the factor that keeps this little blind family

00:18:57.560 --> 00:19:07.880
together.

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:14.360
In a single week the young incredibly increase their weight by ten times apparently just

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:16.600
from drinking her secretion.

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:20.800
Could that be their only food?

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:25.640
As we filmed one of the youngsters revealed a clue to their rapid growth.

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:29.520
It yawned.

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:33.320
It already had hooked teeth like a baby shark.

00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:37.540
It surely doesn't need these if it's going to do nothing but drinking.

00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:41.200
Could it be feeding on something else?

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:50.360
A few hours later our cameras for the first time revealed the answer.

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:52.680
There was a sudden frenzy of activity.

00:19:52.680 --> 00:20:09.760
The babies started swarming all over their mother.

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:14.000
They were tearing at her flanks ripping off segments of her skin.

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:26.520
Skin that proved to be full of fat.

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:32.520
It turned out that she re-grew her skin every three days to provide her young with another

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:39.960
nourishing meal.

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:51.880
Blind, elongated and legless Sicilians may be but simple, inoffensive earthworms they

00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:55.520
are not.

00:20:55.520 --> 00:21:01.760
The most numerous and successful of all amphibians however have kept their legs and developed

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:28.960
them spectacularly.

00:21:28.960 --> 00:21:38.440
Some are walkers.

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:51.000
Others are climbers.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:57.240
There are hoppers.

00:21:57.240 --> 00:22:19.240
There are even gliders who use the membranes on their feet like parachutes.

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:23.920
If their skin is very moist we call these creatures frogs.

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:27.200
If it's less so we call them toads.

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:38.640
They all belong to the same group.

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:46.200
There are some 5,500 different kinds of frogs and toads in the world today.

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:53.880
And here in the leaf litter in this Madagascan forest is the tiniest of them all.

00:22:53.880 --> 00:23:02.480
This is fully adult and in its tiny body which is only a centimetre long is packed a beating

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:07.800
heart, a skeleton, a gut, a brain.

00:23:07.800 --> 00:23:12.680
It's a miracle of miniaturisation.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:19.760
And this basic body plan not only comes in all sizes but many different shapes which

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:28.840
has enabled frogs and toads to colonise all kinds of different environments.

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:48.360
Out of water frogs found a new way to communicate with one another.

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:54.360
Human lungs are comparatively feeble so frogs amplify their calls with cheek or throat pouches

00:23:54.360 --> 00:23:59.640
which act as resonators.

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:08.000
The call of a frog in this South African pool can be heard over a mile away.

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:26.600
It's the painted reed frog, the loudest caller of all for his size.

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:34.280
But a female is not only impressed by the loudness of a male's call.

00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:51.040
She also judges him by how frequently he manages to make that call.

00:24:51.040 --> 00:24:56.240
Calling is a very demanding activity requiring a male to increase his energy consumption

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:58.480
by about 20 times.

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:04.160
So in picking the loudest and fastest caller the female is also selecting the fittest and

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:18.400
most vigorous male as the father of her offspring.

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:26.440
He's the one.

00:25:26.440 --> 00:25:36.560
Success and silence for a few minutes.

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:46.240
In some circumstances however calls need reinforcing with gestures.

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:52.280
The sound of rushing water could drown out the calls of a frog.

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:59.240
Here in this stream in Panama there's a species living alongside that has developed a novel

00:25:59.240 --> 00:26:03.600
way of dealing with that problem.

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:06.520
The rare and wonderful golden frog.

00:26:06.520 --> 00:26:17.760
It does have a voice but it's not loud.

00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:23.080
Individual males set up their territories beside the river and then wait for females

00:26:23.080 --> 00:26:24.760
to turn up.

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:29.920
And since good positions for the territory are not common they may have to hold them

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:33.600
against intruders.

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:37.600
And here one comes.

00:26:37.600 --> 00:26:49.240
But in case his call is inaudible he makes his message clear with a wave.

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:54.040
And his rival waves back.

00:26:54.040 --> 00:27:08.860
He repeats his message so there's no misunderstanding.

00:27:08.860 --> 00:27:12.600
But rival is not deterred.

00:27:12.600 --> 00:27:19.440
Well that makes things perfectly clear.

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:20.440
Another arrives.

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:38.040
But perhaps at last this is a female.

00:27:38.040 --> 00:27:40.240
No it's another male.

00:27:40.240 --> 00:27:47.960
So they will have to be a wrestling match.

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:51.600
That should teach him.

00:27:51.600 --> 00:28:18.640
And his rival signals submission by keeping his head down.

00:28:18.640 --> 00:28:29.560
Now where are those females?

00:28:29.560 --> 00:28:32.400
And here she is.

00:28:32.400 --> 00:28:45.920
She is pure unblemished gold and much bigger than he is.

00:28:45.920 --> 00:28:55.080
While he is fully occupied another challenger arrives.

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:59.080
Since he's already in position there's no point in breaking away for another wrestling

00:28:59.080 --> 00:29:00.080
match.

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:07.940
So he hangs on.

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:14.100
The golden frog has a powerful poison in its skin so it can afford to be conspicuous.

00:29:14.100 --> 00:29:22.640
But most frogs find safety in camouflage.

00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:25.880
This is a South American red-eyed tree frog.

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:33.720
A close match for the leaves on which it habitually sits.

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:36.760
The eggs are not very conspicuous either.

00:29:36.760 --> 00:29:40.580
Just little blobs in transparent jelly.

00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:47.280
And they're always laid over water.

00:29:47.280 --> 00:30:10.560
They develop very rapidly.

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:17.200
In less than a week they become recognizable tadpoles almost ready for freedom.

00:30:17.200 --> 00:30:24.360
Then the jelly liquefies and they simply drop into the water beneath.

00:30:24.360 --> 00:30:32.320
But some don't survive long enough to do so.

00:30:32.320 --> 00:30:51.320
Wasps raid the cluster and carry off the unhatched tadpoles to feed their young.

00:30:51.320 --> 00:31:13.080
But the tadpoles are not entirely helpless.

00:31:13.080 --> 00:31:17.800
By the time they're five days old they know when they're under attack and what's more

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:21.520
they can do something about it.

00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:25.720
There.

00:31:25.720 --> 00:31:37.600
Quick wriggle and the tadpole drops to safety.

00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:55.000
The alarm spreads quickly through the whole cluster and they all take a dive.

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:59.960
Their tails are not yet fully developed but they can swim well enough to take refuge beneath

00:31:59.960 --> 00:32:25.200
the leaves of the water plants.

00:32:25.200 --> 00:32:31.160
So if there's a choice between being carried off by a wasp and taking an early bath there's

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:34.960
no competition.

00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:38.040
But not all frogs abandon their young.

00:32:38.040 --> 00:32:43.640
If you're big enough you can stay and defend them and the male giant African bullfrog is

00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:47.320
as big as a football.

00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:56.520
This pool which formed during the rainy season lies near the margin of a much bigger pond.

00:32:56.520 --> 00:33:01.320
The nursery pool was a good place to lay for it had none of the predators that abound in

00:33:01.320 --> 00:33:04.600
the bigger permanent pond.

00:33:04.600 --> 00:33:13.000
But as the dry season warms up that smaller pool begins to evaporate.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:17.120
Tadpoles are now in real danger.

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:21.800
The other takes action.

00:33:21.800 --> 00:33:37.760
He starts to dig a canal to enable his endangered tadpoles to reach the deeper pond nearby.

00:33:37.760 --> 00:33:42.220
It will be touch and go but if they can only get to the bigger pond they're now vigorous

00:33:42.220 --> 00:33:52.040
enough to have a reasonable chance of survival.

00:33:52.040 --> 00:34:02.280
Break through.

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:18.440
And father leads the way.

00:34:18.440 --> 00:34:24.680
In the rainforests of South America the daily rains create a multitude of tiny pools in

00:34:24.680 --> 00:34:28.140
the centre of many plants.

00:34:28.140 --> 00:34:33.200
This tiny poison arrow frog is carrying his tadpole piggyback.

00:34:33.200 --> 00:34:40.320
It hatched on a leaf and now he's taking it to a pool in a bromeliad high up in the branches.

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:43.560
The tadpole wiggles off.

00:34:43.560 --> 00:34:57.560
He may have half a dozen babies each of which he puts into its own tiny pool.

00:34:57.560 --> 00:35:11.520
He makes regular tours of all his nurseries checking on his tadpole's welfare.

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:16.840
This youngster is hungry and tells himself by nibbling his legs and vibrating against

00:35:16.840 --> 00:35:23.660
his body.

00:35:23.660 --> 00:35:38.040
But the male can't feed the tadpole himself, he needs help.

00:35:38.040 --> 00:35:49.360
He has to find a female.

00:35:49.360 --> 00:35:56.160
There she is.

00:35:56.160 --> 00:36:07.620
He calls.

00:36:07.620 --> 00:36:15.820
And she follows.

00:36:15.820 --> 00:36:23.640
He has to lead for only he knows exactly where he deposited each tadpole.

00:36:23.640 --> 00:36:31.540
This one is now very hungry indeed.

00:36:31.540 --> 00:36:39.640
He calls to the female encouragingly.

00:36:39.640 --> 00:36:47.240
She jumps in perhaps to assess the situation.

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:53.800
Out she comes without having done what's required so he keeps calling.

00:36:53.800 --> 00:36:56.260
In she goes a second time.

00:36:56.260 --> 00:37:00.600
This time she produces food for the hungry tadpole.

00:37:00.600 --> 00:37:03.600
An infertile egg.

00:37:03.600 --> 00:37:05.600
There.

00:37:05.600 --> 00:37:29.600
Out she comes and mother and father embrace.

00:37:29.600 --> 00:37:42.960
Today has its dinner.

00:37:42.960 --> 00:37:48.120
Australia in the southeast has temperate rainforests.

00:37:48.120 --> 00:37:52.960
A cluster of frog's eggs on the damp ground.

00:37:52.960 --> 00:37:57.120
When these hatch the tadpoles will also need a moist nursery.

00:37:57.120 --> 00:38:01.600
Father, a marsupial frog is on guard.

00:38:01.600 --> 00:38:09.800
The eggs are developing fast.

00:38:09.800 --> 00:38:15.040
The male has to keep a careful eye on them for he must be close beside them at the very

00:38:15.040 --> 00:38:24.680
moment when they hatch.

00:38:24.680 --> 00:38:27.240
It's going to be a long wait.

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:40.960
At least 11 days.

00:38:40.960 --> 00:38:46.080
He seems to have decided that the crucial moment has arrived and lowers himself onto

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:52.760
the eggs.

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:59.200
As he does so the tough egg membranes liquefy and the young wriggle free.

00:38:59.200 --> 00:39:05.440
He has two pouches in his skin, one on each hip and the tadpoles start to squirm into

00:39:05.440 --> 00:39:15.800
them.

00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:21.320
Competition between the tadpoles is intense for there are more of them than he can accommodate

00:39:21.320 --> 00:39:48.360
in his pouches.

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:53.480
At last he's taken on board as many as he can manage.

00:39:53.480 --> 00:40:02.000
He will now look after them for up to six weeks.

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:07.240
The young remain in his pouches continuing their development fueled by the remains of

00:40:07.240 --> 00:40:15.520
the yolk in their infant stomachs.

00:40:15.520 --> 00:40:21.060
And then one night his behavior changes.

00:40:21.060 --> 00:40:32.480
His flanks are rippling.

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:55.440
The first of his young is emerging.

00:40:55.440 --> 00:41:10.200
The

00:41:10.200 --> 00:41:17.920
profound transformation that converted a tadpole into this young frog took place entirely within

00:41:17.920 --> 00:41:29.400
its father's moist pouch.

00:41:29.400 --> 00:41:32.640
The parched bush country of southern Africa.

00:41:32.640 --> 00:41:40.960
Here it rains only twice a year and then only briefly.

00:41:40.960 --> 00:41:53.840
But when it does the ground in places erupts.

00:41:53.840 --> 00:42:04.480
Rain frogs as they're aptly called have been waiting for months below ground for this moment.

00:42:04.480 --> 00:42:19.880
After starving for so long they're keen to feed.

00:42:19.880 --> 00:42:47.880
As darkness falls the males begin to call.

00:42:47.880 --> 00:43:09.320
Females are fat with eggs.

00:43:09.320 --> 00:43:14.120
The males are so much smaller that they can't embrace a female.

00:43:14.120 --> 00:43:19.600
So they produce glue from glands on their underside and stick themselves to their partner's

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:22.760
back.

00:43:22.760 --> 00:43:28.880
But sometimes that only results in a chain of enthusiastic but undiscriminating males

00:43:28.880 --> 00:43:38.440
stuck to one another.

00:43:38.440 --> 00:43:41.480
Their brief time above ground has come to an end.

00:43:41.480 --> 00:43:45.160
The female starts to dig.

00:43:45.160 --> 00:43:49.240
The diminutive male being stuck on goes with her.

00:43:49.240 --> 00:44:00.480
He will fertilize the eggs later below ground.

00:44:00.480 --> 00:44:07.840
Her stay on the surface is over.

00:44:07.840 --> 00:44:13.480
The female has excavated a little chamber for herself and below that she's made a second

00:44:13.480 --> 00:44:20.360
one which is filled with a frothy foam.

00:44:20.360 --> 00:44:44.640
This is the nursery for her tadpoles.

00:44:44.640 --> 00:45:00.640
The female stays underground away from the lethal heat for several more weeks.

00:45:00.640 --> 00:45:16.960
By now her offspring have almost completed their time as tadpoles.

00:45:16.960 --> 00:45:24.680
The rains return.

00:45:24.680 --> 00:45:47.800
Below ground the youngsters await their release.

00:45:47.800 --> 00:46:07.480
The female leads the way and her brood are with her.

00:46:07.480 --> 00:46:25.680
Rain is even rarer in Australia.

00:46:25.680 --> 00:46:33.040
There in the central deserts it may not fall for years on end.

00:46:33.040 --> 00:46:36.440
But there are amphibians even here.

00:46:36.440 --> 00:46:42.960
Small toads that remain underground in a state of suspended animation for years just to take

00:46:42.960 --> 00:46:48.560
advantage of a few rainy days.

00:46:48.560 --> 00:46:53.040
After the rains have fallen, spadefoot toads all emerge together.

00:46:53.040 --> 00:47:04.680
They must feed and breed if possible before the sun rises.

00:47:04.680 --> 00:47:10.720
But the desert dries only too quickly even after the heaviest of storms.

00:47:10.720 --> 00:47:13.880
Temperatures rise to 50 degrees centigrade.

00:47:13.880 --> 00:47:16.800
Now water will evaporate instantly.

00:47:16.800 --> 00:47:22.220
This is one of the hottest places on earth.

00:47:22.220 --> 00:47:27.040
So the toads have to retreat once again below ground.

00:47:27.040 --> 00:47:33.040
The miracle is that they're here at all.

00:47:33.040 --> 00:47:40.480
A toad that can live in as parts of the desert as this is impressive evidence of the versatility

00:47:40.480 --> 00:47:42.120
of the amphibians.

00:47:42.120 --> 00:47:48.360
The way they can adapt their behaviour and their anatomy to live so far away from water.

00:47:48.360 --> 00:47:53.360
But there's one group of animals that can really call the desert their own.

00:47:53.360 --> 00:47:55.080
The lizards.

00:47:55.080 --> 00:48:10.040
And we'll look at them in the next episode of Life in Cold Blood.

00:48:10.040 --> 00:48:14.320
Amphibians are the most threatened group of vertebrates on the planet.

00:48:14.320 --> 00:48:22.020
In recent years, a strange and lethal fungal disease has started to spread among them.

00:48:22.020 --> 00:48:27.600
The golden frog, which lives only in one small area in Panama, was in particular danger as

00:48:27.600 --> 00:48:30.980
the disease is already on the frontier of its territory.

00:48:30.980 --> 00:48:37.720
If we were to film it at all, we would have to move quickly.

00:48:37.720 --> 00:48:55.200
For series producer Miles Barton, that meant cutting short Christmas.

00:48:55.200 --> 00:49:00.440
We had been told that in Panama, the frog's few remaining breeding streams were being

00:49:00.440 --> 00:49:06.200
rapidly destroyed by the building of a new road, making the last tiny population even

00:49:06.200 --> 00:49:10.760
more at risk from the disease.

00:49:10.760 --> 00:49:14.600
The fungus clogs the animal's moist skin.

00:49:14.600 --> 00:49:22.240
Since all frogs breathe through their skin, infected animals die from suffocation.

00:49:22.240 --> 00:49:28.120
Frog biologist Eric Lindquist, who first described the golden frog's signalling behaviour, helped

00:49:28.120 --> 00:49:37.280
the film team to thoroughly disinfect their kit before travelling into the frog's territory.

00:49:37.280 --> 00:49:42.280
Freshly scrubbed up, Eric took the team to one of the golden frog's last known breeding

00:49:42.280 --> 00:49:49.000
sites.

00:49:49.000 --> 00:49:52.000
But would they still be there?

00:49:52.000 --> 00:49:58.560
Yeah, you hear that?

00:49:58.560 --> 00:50:01.560
That's a male calling.

00:50:01.560 --> 00:50:06.640
OK, we have another male crawling up over here, crawling up the rock face.

00:50:06.640 --> 00:50:11.600
But with the fungus approaching at a rate of up to 25 miles a year, the frogs were rapidly

00:50:11.600 --> 00:50:15.280
disappearing from all their known breeding sites.

00:50:15.280 --> 00:50:29.160
The advance crew immediately set about filming as much of the behaviour as they could.

00:50:29.160 --> 00:50:41.520
By the time I arrived, there was only one remaining location where the frogs survived.

00:50:41.520 --> 00:50:43.760
Where exactly are we going?

00:50:43.760 --> 00:50:46.720
I would prefer not saying precisely.

00:50:46.720 --> 00:50:52.320
You see, this is really the last population of the golden frog left in the wild.

00:50:52.320 --> 00:50:59.520
And historically, the locals have been collecting out these animals as good luck talismans.

00:50:59.520 --> 00:51:06.160
And so now, left with just one population, I'm concerned that if the secret locality

00:51:06.160 --> 00:51:12.720
gets given out, there will be international collectors that would come.

00:51:12.720 --> 00:51:13.720
Really?

00:51:13.720 --> 00:51:14.720
Sure.

00:51:14.720 --> 00:51:21.240
They're rare enough now where many people would pay top dollar for these animals.

00:51:21.240 --> 00:51:23.480
Were they ever, what you might call, common?

00:51:23.480 --> 00:51:28.800
When I talk to people who had been here in the past, that the populations were so abundant

00:51:28.800 --> 00:51:32.720
that one would have to watch where they're stepping to keep from killing one.

00:51:32.720 --> 00:51:33.720
Really?

00:51:33.720 --> 00:51:34.720
Yes.

00:51:34.720 --> 00:51:42.720
Eric has his own low-tech method of finding them, which he assures me normally works.

00:51:42.720 --> 00:51:56.080
See, when you call, sometimes they'll call back and they'll reveal their location.

00:51:56.080 --> 00:52:00.720
Sometimes they're tucked away behind leaves and they're really difficult to find.

00:52:00.720 --> 00:52:09.120
Hopefully we can elicit a response.

00:52:09.120 --> 00:52:15.920
That's the fastest way to get them to shut up.

00:52:15.920 --> 00:52:16.920
Was that him?

00:52:16.920 --> 00:52:17.920
Yeah, that's him.

00:52:17.920 --> 00:52:18.920
Say they're here.

00:52:18.920 --> 00:52:19.920
They're here.

00:52:19.920 --> 00:52:20.920
There's one over here.

00:52:20.920 --> 00:52:21.920
See him right there?

00:52:21.920 --> 00:52:22.920
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:52:22.920 --> 00:52:23.920
Looks like a male.

00:52:23.920 --> 00:52:24.920
I can do it again.

00:52:24.920 --> 00:52:50.640
You have to hum and whistle at the same time.

00:52:50.640 --> 00:53:07.880
Now we knew the frogs were still here, we could complete the filming.

00:53:07.880 --> 00:53:12.960
The local people have always treasured their remarkable little frog, but Eric was the first

00:53:12.960 --> 00:53:15.240
to document its signalling behaviour.

00:53:15.240 --> 00:53:17.800
It was an animal that was just walking.

00:53:17.800 --> 00:53:23.400
He wasn't sure if the animal was trying to flush out prey or if it was using it in a

00:53:23.400 --> 00:53:25.200
communication role.

00:53:25.200 --> 00:53:30.520
And so a group of us set out to look at whether or not this was communication.

00:53:30.520 --> 00:53:35.300
We tried mirror presentations to the animals and when you presented them with a mirror,

00:53:35.300 --> 00:53:38.920
they would hand wave at the mirror, as opposed to say maybe the backside of a mirror that

00:53:38.920 --> 00:53:42.300
didn't have a reflective surface.

00:53:42.300 --> 00:53:48.080
Some of us have looked specifically at an LCD screen, a little television with a hand

00:53:48.080 --> 00:53:55.880
waving semaphore frog and has elicited a number of responses, specifically from males.

00:53:55.880 --> 00:53:59.020
When you show a television picture to a male and he waves back.

00:53:59.020 --> 00:54:03.320
He waves back and he'll even call to the male on the television screen.

00:54:03.320 --> 00:54:07.560
It's really fascinating.

00:54:07.560 --> 00:54:13.320
We then experimented with a life-size plastic model, complete with waving arm, the sort

00:54:13.320 --> 00:54:33.120
of high-tech gear I thought I might manage to operate myself.

00:54:33.120 --> 00:54:41.040
It's not as easy as you might think.

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:43.480
Eric showed me how it should be done.

00:54:43.480 --> 00:54:50.680
You've got to get that slow motion wave just right.

00:54:50.680 --> 00:55:01.840
The frogs waved.

00:55:01.840 --> 00:55:04.520
They called.

00:55:04.520 --> 00:55:09.560
They even attacked.

00:55:09.560 --> 00:55:13.560
So that wave really is a form of communication.

00:55:13.560 --> 00:55:16.040
So they're just saying keep off, keep off.

00:55:16.040 --> 00:55:17.040
Huh?

00:55:17.040 --> 00:55:18.040
Is that right?

00:55:18.040 --> 00:55:19.120
We're not sure.

00:55:19.120 --> 00:55:24.400
Sometimes there seem to be certain hand waves that may indicate appeasement, showing that

00:55:24.400 --> 00:55:27.640
I'm just walking through perhaps your territory.

00:55:27.640 --> 00:55:28.640
Don't bother me.

00:55:28.640 --> 00:55:29.640
Really?

00:55:29.640 --> 00:55:30.640
Yeah.

00:55:30.640 --> 00:55:31.640
Please.

00:55:31.640 --> 00:55:37.120
But how endangered is the golden frog?

00:55:37.120 --> 00:55:38.400
This is it.

00:55:38.400 --> 00:55:46.080
What you see, you're going to be the last crew to film these in the wild.

00:55:46.080 --> 00:55:48.080
And indeed we were.

00:55:48.080 --> 00:55:53.960
Soon after finishing filming, the local scientists decided the time had come to take all the

00:55:53.960 --> 00:56:00.400
surviving golden frogs into captivity before the fungus arrives here and kills them all.

00:56:00.400 --> 00:56:05.960
They and other rare species of frog also threatened were being brought back to a special frog

00:56:05.960 --> 00:56:13.080
hospital where I was introduced to some of the other patients.

00:56:13.080 --> 00:56:18.400
Here they're being treated daily with a fungicide, but without a vaccine to protect them and

00:56:18.400 --> 00:56:23.320
with the fungus still at large in the forest, they can't be reintroduced into their proper

00:56:23.320 --> 00:56:29.560
home.

00:56:29.560 --> 00:56:34.120
Links so common in these humid forests are crucial links in the ecology.

00:56:34.120 --> 00:56:39.080
If they disappear, all kinds of food chains will be broken and the effect could be little

00:56:39.080 --> 00:56:44.440
short of catastrophic to wildlife in general.

00:56:44.440 --> 00:56:49.640
And sadly, for now at least, it seems that the golden frog has waved its last in the

00:56:49.640 --> 00:57:08.140
wild.

00:57:08.140 --> 00:57:10.140
You
